The split of libertarians has been from the beginning&#151;anarchists vs. minarchists.

And serendipity strikes. I can cite an article from today at LRC:

[http://www.lewrockwell.com/kinsella/kinsella15.html What It Means To Be an Anarcho-Capitalist] by N. Stephan Kinsella.

Teaser:
:Libertarian opponents of anarchy are attacking a straw man. Their arguments are usually utilitarian in nature and amount to "but anarchy won’t work" or "we need the (things provided by the) state." But these attacks are confused at best, if not disingenuous. To be an anarchist does not mean you think anarchy will "work" (whatever that means); nor that you predict it will or "can" be achieved. It is possible to be a pessimistic anarchist, after all. To be an anarchist only means that you believe that aggression is not justified, and that states necessarily employ aggression. And, therefore, that states, and the aggression they necessarily employ, are unjustified. It’s quite simple, really. It’s an ethical view, so no surprise it confuses utilitarians.

I also summarize part of it so:

:Ancaps are anti aggression, thus anti state.
:Anyone not ancap, supports aggression.
:Regular crime cannot be completely stopped,
:but that does not stop our condemning it,
:nor our attempt to eradicate it.
:The state may not be completely stopped,
:but that should not stop our condemning it,
:nor our attempt to eradicate it.

Thus in this sense, the minarchists by definition support aggression. As shown in the [[Non-Aggression Principle]], that is the antithesis of civilization.

The Libertarian Party is the center of the libertarian political effort. All its political activities are supposed to be for show and thus only for their educational value. But nonetheless, there are many minarchists on board who take that running for office somewhat more sincerely than show. And in that these are running after that golden ring, corruption has, as usual, raised its ugly head (see Bergland Browne Willis Jacob Hornberger battle).

Many of the ancappers left long ago as well as a variety of not quite ancappers. Many think politics is just not the way to go. For education, there are more direct ways, and for winning office, most ancappers say no thank you please, I have ZAP to keep me warm.

All that does not leave a whole lot left over for any strategies that involve politics. Even voting is considered immoral in that it tends to give the appearance of legitimacy to the state. But, following Rothbard's ideas, there might be space for [[defensive voting]] and for [[brokering swing-votes]].

The 'nay-sayers' hold that it's all immoral, a waste of time, and tends to corrupt anyone involved. And the 'yea-sayers' don't seem to have organized much of anything significant.

But, stay tuned. As this section gets more work done on it, there will be telling links to both sides of this issue.


